% 49-differential-testing.weft — Differential testing against clang.
%
% Each program is compiled by BOTH kcc and clang, linked freestanding,
% run, and their exit codes compared.  clang is the oracle: any
% disagreement on a well-defined program is a kcc code-generation bug.

\chapter{Differential Testing}
\label{ch:differential-testing}

The code-generation suite of Chapter~\ref{ch:codegen-tests} checks each
program against an exit code computed by hand.  That is a hard ceiling:
a test can only be as complex as a value one is willing to work out on
paper.  Differential testing lifts the ceiling.  It compiles each
program with \kcc{} \emph{and} with \texttt{clang}, runs both, and
compares---using \texttt{clang} as an oracle for what the program
\emph{should} do.  No expected value is written down; the reference
compiler supplies it.  This is how the self-hosting tokenizer of
Chapter~\ref{ch:self-hosting-lexer} was validated, generalised here into
a standing suite.

The programs are freestanding: each is an \texttt{int main(void)} whose
return value becomes the process exit code, linked with entry
\texttt{\_main} and no C runtime---the same shape \kcc{}'s own tests
use, and one \texttt{clang} honours too.  Because an exit code is a
single byte, programs return small results (or reduce modulo a small
number); a genuine miscompilation practically never lands on an exact
multiple of~256 away from the correct answer, so agreement on the byte
is agreement in fact.

Each program lands in one of three buckets.  \textbf{ok}: both compilers
agree.  \textbf{mismatch}: both compile and run but disagree---a
code-generation defect, the bug this suite exists to catch.
\textbf{gap}: \texttt{clang} compiles it but \kcc{} cannot---a missing
language feature, reported but not counted as a failure, since it maps
the frontier of what \kcc{} accepts rather than a wrong answer.

\section{The Runner}

The runner compiles with the oracle first: if \texttt{clang} rejects a
program the program itself is wrong, and it is skipped.  Otherwise it
records \texttt{clang}'s exit code, then tries \kcc{}.  A \kcc{}
compile failure is a gap; a successful compile is linked, run, and its
exit code compared against the oracle's.

@d Difftest: runner @{
#!/bin/bash
# kcc differential test suite (vs clang) — generated by weft, do not edit
set -u

PASS=0
FAIL=0
GAP=0
XFAIL=0
TOTAL=0
KCC="tmp/kcc"
TMP="/tmp/kcc_difftest_$$"
SDK=$(xcrun --show-sdk-path 2>/dev/null)

mkdir -p "$TMP"
trap 'rm -rf "$TMP"' EXIT

# Link an object freestanding and run it; echo the exit code, or "L" if the
# link fails (kcc emitted an object the system linker rejects).
link_run() {
    if ld "$1" -o "$2" -lSystem -syslibroot "$SDK" -e _main -arch arm64 2>/dev/null; then
        "$2" >/dev/null 2>&1
        echo $?
    else
        echo "L"
    fi
}

difftest() {
    local name="$1"
    local src="$2"
    TOTAL=$((TOTAL + 1))
    printf '%s\n' "$src" > "$TMP/d.c"

    # Oracle: clang must accept the program, else it is not valid C.
    if ! clang -c "$TMP/d.c" -o "$TMP/dc.o" 2>/dev/null; then
        printf "  %-28s SKIP (clang rejects)\n" "$name"
        return
    fi
    local crc
    crc=$(link_run "$TMP/dc.o" "$TMP/dc")

    # kcc: a compile failure is a feature gap, not a wrong answer.
    if ! $KCC -c "$TMP/d.c" -o "$TMP/dk.o" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        GAP=$((GAP + 1))
        printf "  %-28s GAP (kcc cannot compile)\n" "$name"
        return
    fi
    local krc
    krc=$(link_run "$TMP/dk.o" "$TMP/dk")

    if [ "$krc" = "$crc" ]; then
        PASS=$((PASS + 1))
        printf "  %-28s ok (%s)\n" "$name" "$krc"
    else
        FAIL=$((FAIL + 1))
        printf "  %-28s MISMATCH kcc=%s clang=%s\n" "$name" "$krc" "$crc"
    fi
}

# Like difftest, but the divergence is a documented, expected limitation.
# It is reported but never fails the build.  If it unexpectedly agrees, the
# limitation has been resolved and the case should graduate to difftest.
difftest_xfail() {
    local name="$1"
    local src="$2"
    local reason="$3"
    TOTAL=$((TOTAL + 1))
    printf '%s\n' "$src" > "$TMP/d.c"
    if ! clang -c "$TMP/d.c" -o "$TMP/dc.o" 2>/dev/null; then
        printf "  %-28s SKIP (clang rejects)\n" "$name"
        return
    fi
    local crc
    crc=$(link_run "$TMP/dc.o" "$TMP/dc")
    if ! $KCC -c "$TMP/d.c" -o "$TMP/dk.o" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        GAP=$((GAP + 1))
        printf "  %-28s GAP (kcc cannot compile)\n" "$name"
        return
    fi
    local krc
    krc=$(link_run "$TMP/dk.o" "$TMP/dk")
    if [ "$krc" = "$crc" ]; then
        printf "  %-28s XPASS (divergence resolved: %s)\n" "$name" "$reason"
    else
        XFAIL=$((XFAIL + 1))
        printf "  %-28s xfail: %s (kcc=%s clang=%s)\n" "$name" "$reason" "$krc" "$crc"
    fi
}

echo "kcc differential test suite (vs clang)"
echo "======================================"
@}

\section{Arithmetic and Bit Manipulation}

Integer arithmetic is where an all-spill backend's shuttling of values
through scratch registers is most likely to drop or corrupt a value.
These programs stress operator precedence, signed and unsigned division
and remainder (well-defined truncation toward zero in C), shifts, and
the bitwise operators---keeping every result in defined territory.

@d Difftest: arithmetic @{
echo ""
echo "--- Arithmetic and Bits ---"

difftest "precedence-1" \
    "int main(void){ return 2 + 3 * 4 - 6 / 2; }"
difftest "precedence-2" \
    "int main(void){ return (2 + 3) * (4 - 1) % 7; }"
difftest "signed-div-neg" \
    "int main(void){ int a = -17; int b = 5; return 100 + a / b; }"
difftest "signed-mod-neg" \
    "int main(void){ int a = -17; int b = 5; return 100 + a % b; }"
difftest "unsigned-div" \
    "int main(void){ unsigned int a = 4000000000; unsigned int b = 7; return (int)(a / b % 200); }"
difftest "shifts" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 1; return (a << 5) + (256 >> 3); }"
difftest "bitwise-mix" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 0xF0; int b = 0x3C; return (a & b) | (a ^ b); }"
difftest "chained-cmp" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 5; int b = 7; int c = 5; return (a < b) + (a == c) + (b > c); }"
difftest "unary-neg-not" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 5; return (-a + 10) + (~a & 7) + (!0); }"
@}

\section{Control Flow}

Branch lowering, loop back-edges, and \texttt{switch} dispatch are
exercised here: nested conditionals, each loop form, fall-through, and
\texttt{break}/\texttt{continue}.

@d Difftest: control @{
echo ""
echo "--- Control Flow ---"

difftest "nested-if" \
    "int main(void){ int x = 7; if (x > 5) { if (x < 10) return 1; return 2; } return 3; }"
difftest "while-sum" \
    "int main(void){ int s = 0; int i = 1; while (i <= 10) { s = s + i; i = i + 1; } return s; }"
difftest "do-while" \
    "int main(void){ int n = 0; int i = 0; do { n = n + 2; i = i + 1; } while (i < 6); return n; }"
difftest "for-product" \
    "int main(void){ int p = 1; int i = 1; for (i = 1; i <= 5; i = i + 1) p = p * i; return p % 200; }"
difftest "switch-fallthrough" \
    "int main(void){ int x = 2; int r = 0; switch (x) { case 1: r = r + 1; case 2: r = r + 10; case 3: r = r + 100; break; default: r = 255; } return r; }"
difftest "break-continue" \
    "int main(void){ int s = 0; int i = 0; while (i < 20) { i = i + 1; if (i == 5) continue; if (i == 12) break; s = s + i; } return s; }"
difftest "nested-loops" \
    "int main(void){ int c = 0; int i = 0; for (i = 0; i < 5; i = i + 1) { int j = 0; for (j = 0; j < 5; j = j + 1) c = c + 1; } return c; }"
@}

\section{Functions and Recursion}

Calling convention, argument passing, and return values are exercised
by recursion (single and mutual) and multi-argument calls.

@d Difftest: functions @{
echo ""
echo "--- Functions and Recursion ---"

difftest "factorial" \
    "int fact(int n){ if (n <= 1) return 1; return n * fact(n - 1); } int main(void){ return fact(5) % 200; }"
difftest "fib" \
    "int fib(int n){ if (n < 2) return n; return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2); } int main(void){ return fib(11); }"
difftest "mutual-rec" \
    "int is_even(int n); int is_odd(int n){ if (n == 0) return 0; return is_even(n - 1); } int is_even(int n){ if (n == 0) return 1; return is_odd(n - 1); } int main(void){ return is_even(10) + is_odd(7); }"
difftest "many-args" \
    "int sum6(int a, int b, int c, int d, int e, int f){ return a + b + c + d + e + f; } int main(void){ return sum6(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6); }"
difftest "accumulate" \
    "int step(int acc, int x){ return acc + x * x; } int main(void){ int acc = 0; int i = 1; while (i <= 6) { acc = step(acc, i); i = i + 1; } return acc % 250; }"
@}

\section{Pointers, Arrays, and Structs}

Aggregate access and pointer arithmetic---subscripting, member access
through pointers, address-of, and passing addresses to functions---%
are the patterns most sensitive to the address-computation paths.

@d Difftest: aggregates @{
echo ""
echo "--- Pointers, Arrays, Structs ---"

difftest "array-sum" \
    "int main(void){ int a[6]; int i = 0; for (i = 0; i < 6; i = i + 1) a[i] = i * i; int s = 0; for (i = 0; i < 6; i = i + 1) s = s + a[i]; return s; }"
difftest "array-init-list" \
    "int main(void){ int a[5] = {3, 1, 4, 1, 5}; return a[0] + a[2] + a[4]; }"
difftest "pointer-swap" \
    "void swap(int *a, int *b){ int t = *a; *a = *b; *b = t; } int main(void){ int x = 10; int y = 3; swap(&x, &y); return x - y; }"
difftest "struct-fields" \
    "struct P { int x; int y; }; int main(void){ struct P p; p.x = 12; p.y = 30; return p.x + p.y; }"
difftest "struct-ptr" \
    "struct P { int x; int y; }; int dist(struct P *p){ return p->x + p->y; } int main(void){ struct P p; p.x = 40; p.y = 2; return dist(&p); }"
difftest "struct-array" \
    "struct P { int a; int b; }; int main(void){ struct P v[3]; int i = 0; for (i = 0; i < 3; i = i + 1) { v[i].a = i; v[i].b = i * 10; } return v[2].a + v[2].b; }"
difftest "addr-of-elem" \
    "void set(int *p, int v){ *p = v; } int main(void){ int a[4]; int i = 0; for (i = 0; i < 4; i = i + 1) set(&a[i], i + 1); return a[0] + a[1] + a[2] + a[3]; }"
difftest "ptr-to-ptr" \
    "int main(void){ int x = 42; int *p = &x; int **pp = &p; **pp = 7; return x; }"
difftest "array-2d" \
    "int main(void){ int a[3][3]; int i; int j; for (i = 0; i < 3; i = i + 1) for (j = 0; j < 3; j = j + 1) a[i][j] = i * 3 + j; return a[2][2]; }"
difftest "array-2d-nonsquare" \
    "int main(void){ int a[2][5]; int i; int j; for (i = 0; i < 2; i = i + 1) for (j = 0; j < 5; j = j + 1) a[i][j] = i * 5 + j; return a[1][4]; }"
difftest "array-3d" \
    "int main(void){ int a[2][2][2]; a[1][1][1] = 42; a[0][1][0] = 5; return a[1][1][1] + a[0][1][0]; }"
difftest "matrix-multiply" \
    "int main(void){ int a[2][2]; int b[2][2]; a[0][0]=1; a[0][1]=2; a[1][0]=3; a[1][1]=4; b[0][0]=5; b[0][1]=6; b[1][0]=7; b[1][1]=8; int c = a[0][0]*b[0][0] + a[0][1]*b[1][0]; return c; }"
difftest "sizeof-2d-array" \
    "int main(void){ int a[3][4]; return (int)(sizeof(a) / sizeof(a[0][0])); }"
difftest "sret-basic" \
    "struct P { int x; int y; }; struct P make(int a, int b){ struct P p; p.x = a; p.y = b; return p; } int main(void){ struct P q = make(15, 27); return q.x + q.y; }"
difftest "sret-zero-arg" \
    "struct P { int x; int y; }; struct P make(void){ struct P p; p.x = 10; p.y = 20; return p; } int main(void){ struct P q = make(); return q.x + q.y; }"
difftest "sret-three-fields" \
    "struct P { int a; int b; int c; }; struct P make(void){ struct P p; p.a = 1; p.b = 2; p.c = 3; return p; } int main(void){ struct P q = make(); return q.a + q.b + q.c; }"
difftest "sret-assign" \
    "struct P { int x; int y; }; struct P make(int a){ struct P p; p.x = a; p.y = a * 2; return p; } int main(void){ struct P q; q = make(7); return q.x + q.y; }"
difftest "sret-assign-global" \
    "struct P { int x; int y; }; struct P g; struct P make(int a){ struct P p; p.x = a; p.y = a * 3; return p; } int main(void){ g = make(5); return g.x + g.y; }"
difftest "sret-member-of-temp" \
    "struct P { int x; int y; }; struct P make(void){ struct P p; p.x = 5; p.y = 9; return p; } int main(void){ return make().y; }"
difftest "sret-passthrough" \
    "struct P { int x; int y; }; struct P mk(int a){ struct P p; p.x = a; p.y = a + 1; return p; } struct P wrap(int a){ return mk(a); } int main(void){ struct P q = wrap(20); return q.x + q.y; }"
difftest "sret-five-args" \
    "struct P { int x; int y; }; struct P make(int a, int b, int c, int d, int e){ struct P p; p.x = a + b + c; p.y = d + e; return p; } int main(void){ struct P q = make(1, 2, 3, 4, 5); return q.x + q.y; }"
difftest "sarg-basic" \
    "struct P { int x; int y; }; int sum(struct P p){ return p.x + p.y; } int main(void){ struct P q; q.x = 3; q.y = 4; return sum(q); }"
difftest "sarg-value-isolation" \
    "struct P { int x; int y; }; int f(struct P p){ p.x = 99; return p.x + p.y; } int main(void){ struct P q; q.x = 3; q.y = 4; int r = f(q); return r + q.x; }"
difftest "sarg-three-fields" \
    "struct P { int a; int b; int c; }; int sum(struct P p){ return p.a + p.b + p.c; } int main(void){ struct P q; q.a = 1; q.b = 2; q.c = 3; return sum(q); }"
difftest "sarg-two-structs" \
    "struct P { int x; int y; }; int add(struct P a, struct P b){ return a.x + a.y + b.x + b.y; } int main(void){ struct P p; p.x = 1; p.y = 2; struct P q; q.x = 3; q.y = 4; return add(p, q); }"
difftest "sarg-mixed" \
    "struct P { int x; int y; }; int f(int a, struct P p, int b){ return a + p.x + p.y + b; } int main(void){ struct P q; q.x = 5; q.y = 6; return f(10, q, 20); }"
difftest "sarg-pass-and-return" \
    "struct P { int x; int y; }; struct P dbl(struct P p){ struct P r; r.x = p.x * 2; r.y = p.y * 2; return r; } int main(void){ struct P q; q.x = 3; q.y = 4; struct P d = dbl(q); return d.x + d.y; }"
difftest "sarg-temp" \
    "struct P { int x; int y; }; struct P mk(int a){ struct P p; p.x = a; p.y = a + 1; return p; } int use(struct P p){ return p.x + p.y; } int main(void){ return use(mk(10)); }"
@}

The floating-point cases exercise the \texttt{double} pipeline end to
end (\S\ref{sec:parse-float}): compile-time constant evaluation, the
four arithmetic operations, ordered comparisons, and conversions in
both directions, including through variables, globals, arrays, loops,
and function boundaries.  Every observable value is an \texttt{int}
(the exit code), so the truncating \texttt{(int)} cast is always the
final step.

@d Difftest: floating point @{
echo ""
echo "--- Floating Point ---"

difftest "fp-const-arith" \
    "int main(void){ double a = 1.5; double b = 2.25; return (int)((a + b) * 2); }"
difftest "fp-sub-scale" \
    "int main(void){ double a = 3.5, b = 1.25; return (int)((a - b) * 100); }"
difftest "fp-div" \
    "int main(void){ double a = 10.0, b = 4.0; return (int)(a / b * 10); }"
difftest "fp-int-to-double" \
    "int main(void){ int n = 7; double d = n; return (int)(d * 2); }"
difftest "fp-double-to-int" \
    "int main(void){ double d = 9.87; return (int)d; }"
difftest "fp-mixed-promote" \
    "int main(void){ int n = 3; double r = n + 1.5; return (int)(r * 2); }"
difftest "fp-compare" \
    "int main(void){ double a = 1.1; double b = 2.2; return a < b ? 10 : 20; }"
difftest "fp-assign-convert" \
    "int main(void){ double d; int n = 5; d = n; int i; double e = 7.9; i = e; return (int)(d * 3) + i; }"
difftest "fp-global" \
    "double g = 3.5; int main(void){ g = g + 1.5; return (int)g; }"
difftest "fp-array" \
    "int main(void){ double a[3]; a[0] = 1.5; a[1] = 2.5; a[2] = 3.0; return (int)(a[0] + a[1] + a[2]); }"
difftest "fp-loop-accumulate" \
    "int main(void){ double s = 0.0; int i; for (i = 0; i < 5; i = i + 1) s = s + 1.5; return (int)s; }"
difftest "fp-exponent-literal" \
    "int main(void){ double d = 1.5e3; return (int)(d / 100); }"
difftest "fp-function" \
    "int scale(double x){ return (int)(x * 2); } int main(void){ return scale(3.5); }"
difftest "fp-return" \
    "double half(double x){ return x / 2.0; } int main(void){ return (int)(half(9.0) * 2); }"
@}

\section{Operators and Edge Cases}

The remaining operators---ternary, comma, compound assignment,
increment/decrement, short-circuit logicals---and a few numeric edge
cases round out the corpus.

@d Difftest: operators @{
echo ""
echo "--- Operators and Edge Cases ---"

difftest "ternary-chain" \
    "int main(void){ int x = 3; return x == 1 ? 10 : x == 2 ? 20 : x == 3 ? 30 : 40; }"
difftest "comma" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 0; int b = (a = 5, a + 3); return a + b; }"
difftest "compound-assign" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 10; a += 5; a *= 2; a -= 3; a /= 4; return a; }"
difftest "inc-dec" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 5; int b = a++; int c = ++a; return a + b + c; }"
difftest "short-circuit" \
    "int side(int *n){ *n = *n + 1; return 1; } int main(void){ int n = 0; if (0 && side(&n)) n = n + 100; if (1 || side(&n)) n = n + 10; return n; }"
difftest "sizeof-mix" \
    "int main(void){ return sizeof(int) + sizeof(char) + sizeof(long); }"
difftest "char-arith" \
    "int main(void){ char c = 'A'; int d = c + 1; return d; }"
difftest "global-counter" \
    "int g = 100; void bump(void){ g = g + 7; } int main(void){ bump(); bump(); bump(); return g % 200; }"
difftest "static-local" \
    "int next(void){ static int c = 0; c = c + 1; return c; } int main(void){ next(); next(); return next() + next(); }"
@}

\section{Deeper Combinations}

A second batch reaches for feature \emph{combinations}: signed
comparisons and shifts of negatives, whole-\texttt{struct} assignment,
nested structs, arrays of pointers, multi-level indirection, register
pressure from many live locals, explicit narrowing casts, and a
function pointer stored in a \texttt{struct} field---the last a known
frontier that surfaces as a gap.

@d Difftest: combinations @{
echo ""
echo "--- Deeper Combinations ---"

difftest "neg-compare" \
    "int main(void){ int a = -5; int b = 3; return (a < b) * 100 + (a <= b) * 10 + (b > a); }"
difftest "ashr-negative" \
    "int main(void){ int a = -64; return (a >> 2) + 200; }"
difftest "mod-signs" \
    "int main(void){ return (17 % 5) + (-17 % 5) + (17 % -5) + 100; }"
difftest "nested-struct" \
    "struct I { int x; int y; }; struct O { struct I p; int z; }; int main(void){ struct O o; o.p.x = 1; o.p.y = 2; o.z = 3; return o.p.x + o.p.y + o.z; }"
difftest "struct-assign" \
    "struct P { int a; int b; int c; }; int main(void){ struct P x; x.a = 5; x.b = 6; x.c = 7; struct P y; y = x; return y.a + y.b + y.c; }"
difftest "array-of-ptr" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 1; int b = 2; int c = 3; int *p[3]; p[0] = &a; p[1] = &b; p[2] = &c; int s = 0; int i = 0; while (i < 3) { s = s + *p[i]; i = i + 1; } return s; }"
difftest "triple-deref" \
    "int main(void){ int x = 9; int *p = &x; int **q = &p; int ***r = &q; ***r = 42; return x; }"
difftest "deep-arith" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 2; int b = 3; int c = 4; int d = 5; return ((a + b) * (c + d) - (a * b)) / (c - a) % 50; }"
difftest "many-locals" \
    "int main(void){ int a=1; int b=2; int c=3; int d=4; int e=5; int f=6; int g=7; int h=8; int i=9; int j=10; return a+b+c+d+e+f+g+h+i+j; }"
difftest "string-index" \
    "int main(void){ char *s = \"hello\"; return s[0] + s[4]; }"
difftest "cast-narrow" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 300; char c = (char)a; return (int)c + 100; }"
difftest "fnptr-in-struct" \
    "struct Ops { int (*f)(int, int); }; int add(int a, int b){ return a + b; } int main(void){ struct Ops o; o.f = add; return o.f(20, 22); }"
@}

\section{Language Features}

A broad sweep across language constructs, several of which the harness
first flagged as bugs and which are now fixed: enumerators with explicit
values, \texttt{sizeof} of a whole array (which must not decay to a
pointer), the comma operator in a \texttt{for} clause (a parser register
bug that hung the loop), and pointer difference.  The rest exercise
\texttt{goto}, dense \texttt{switch}, deep recursion, eight-argument
calls, pointer comparison, and a spread of operators.

@d Difftest: language @{
echo ""
echo "--- Language Features ---"

difftest "enum-explicit" \
    "enum E { A, B, C, D = 10, E2 }; int main(void){ return A + B + C + D + E2; }"
difftest "enum-negative" \
    "enum E { X = -1, Y, Z = 100, W }; int main(void){ return X + Y + Z + W + 100; }"
difftest "sizeof-array-count" \
    "int main(void){ int a[10]; return (int)(sizeof(a) / sizeof(a[0])); }"
difftest "sizeof-array-bytes" \
    "int main(void){ char a[25]; return (int)sizeof(a); }"
difftest "for-comma-update" \
    "int main(void){ int s = 0; int i; for (i = 0; i < 5; i = i + 1, s = s + 10) { } return s; }"
difftest "for-comma-both" \
    "int main(void){ int s = 0; int i; int j; for (i = 0, j = 10; i < j; i = i + 1, j = j - 1) { s = s + 1; } return s; }"
difftest "ptr-diff-index" \
    "int main(void){ int a[10]; int *p = &a[2]; int *q = &a[7]; return (int)(q - p); }"
difftest "ptr-diff-strlen" \
    "int slen(char *s){ char *p = s; while (*p) p = p + 1; return (int)(p - s); } int main(void){ return slen(\"hello world\"); }"
difftest "goto-loop" \
    "int main(void){ int i = 0; int s = 0; loop: if (i >= 10) goto end; s = s + i; i = i + 1; goto loop; end: return s; }"
difftest "goto-forward" \
    "int main(void){ int x = 5; if (x > 0) goto pos; return 1; pos: return 42; }"
difftest "goto-cleanup" \
    "int f(int n){ int r = 0; if (n < 0) goto done; r = n * 2; done: return r; } int main(void){ return f(21); }"
difftest "goto-break-out" \
    "int main(void){ int s = 0; int i = 0; while (i < 5) { if (i == 3) goto out; s = s + i; i = i + 1; } out: return s + 10; }"
difftest "switch-dense" \
    "int f(int x){ switch (x) { case 0: return 10; case 1: return 20; case 2: return 30; case 3: return 40; default: return 99; } } int main(void){ return f(2) + f(5); }"
difftest "nested-switch" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 1; int b = 2; int r = 0; switch (a) { case 1: switch (b) { case 2: r = 7; break; default: r = 1; } break; default: r = 0; } return r; }"
difftest "ackermann" \
    "int ack(int m, int n){ if (m == 0) return n + 1; if (n == 0) return ack(m - 1, 1); return ack(m - 1, ack(m, n - 1)); } int main(void){ return ack(2, 3); }"
difftest "eight-args" \
    "int f(int a, int b, int c, int d, int e, int g, int h, int i){ return a + b + c + d + e + g + h + i; } int main(void){ return f(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8); }"
difftest "ptr-compare" \
    "int main(void){ int a[5]; int *p = a; int *e = a + 5; int c = 0; while (p < e) { c = c + 1; p = p + 1; } return c; }"
difftest "assign-chain" \
    "int main(void){ int a; int b; int c; a = b = c = 7; return a + b + c; }"
difftest "hex-octal" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 0xFF; int b = 0777; return a + b; }"
difftest "char-escapes" \
    "int main(void){ char n = '\\n'; char t = '\\t'; char z = '\\0'; return n + t + z; }"
@}

\section{Integer Width}
\label{sec:difftest-width}

The first divergence this suite caught was a real one and is now fixed.
\kcc{}'s all-spill model keeps every value in a 64-bit slot and computes
with 64-bit instructions, so it once left an \texttt{int} or
\texttt{unsigned int} result un-truncated: a computation that wrapped
past $2^{32}$ kept the extra high bits and disagreed with
\texttt{clang}.  \texttt{\_kcc\_ir\_norm32} re-canonicalises the result of each
width-growing operation to its declared width---sign-extending a signed
result, zero-extending an unsigned one.  It is called from all three
sites that produce such results: ordinary binary operators
(\texttt{build\_binary}), compound assignment for both locals and
globals (\texttt{build\_assign}), and unary negation and complement
(\texttt{build\_unary}).  The tests below pin the behaviour down:
unsigned wraparound on \texttt{+}, \texttt{-}, \texttt{*}, \texttt{<<},
compound \texttt{+=}/\texttt{*=}, and unary \texttt{-}/\texttt{\~{}};
unsigned comparison and division after a wrap; the
usual-arithmetic-conversion case where a signed operand meets an
unsigned one; and---as guards against over-eager masking---%
\texttt{long} and pointer computations that must stay 64-bit.  The
\texttt{xfail} machinery remains for the next genuine limitation; at
present there are none.

@d Difftest: divergences @{
echo ""
echo "--- Integer Width (32-bit truncation) ---"

difftest "u32-wrap-sub" \
    "int main(void){ unsigned int a = 0; a = a - 1; return (int)(a % 251); }"
difftest "u32-wrap-add" \
    "int main(void){ unsigned int a = 0xFFFFFFFF; a = a + 1; return (int)(a % 100); }"
difftest "u32-overflow-sum" \
    "int main(void){ unsigned int a = 4000000000; unsigned int b = 1000000000; return (int)((a + b) % 1000); }"
difftest "u32-mul-overflow" \
    "int main(void){ unsigned int a = 100000; unsigned int b = 100000; return (int)((a * b) % 1000); }"
difftest "u32-shl-overflow" \
    "int main(void){ unsigned int a = 0xFFFFFFFF; return (int)((a << 4) % 100); }"
difftest "u32-cmp-after-wrap" \
    "int main(void){ unsigned int a = 0; a = a - 1; unsigned int b = 5; return a > b; }"
difftest "uac-signed-unsigned" \
    "int main(void){ int a = -1; unsigned int b = 10; return (int)((a + b) % 100); }"
difftest "u32-compound-add" \
    "int main(void){ unsigned int a = 0xFFFFFFFF; a += 1; return (int)(a % 100); }"
difftest "u32-compound-mul" \
    "int main(void){ unsigned int a = 100000; a *= 100000; return (int)(a % 1000); }"
difftest "u32-compound-global" \
    "unsigned int g; int main(void){ g = 0xFFFFFFFF; g += 1; return (int)(g % 100); }"
difftest "u32-negate" \
    "int main(void){ unsigned int a = 5; unsigned int b = -a; return (int)(b % 251); }"
difftest "u32-complement" \
    "int main(void){ unsigned int a = 0; unsigned int b = ~a; return (int)(b % 251); }"
difftest "long-stays-64" \
    "int main(void){ long a = 4000000000; long b = 1000000000; return (int)((a + b) % 1000); }"
difftest "ptr-arith-untouched" \
    "int main(void){ int a[10]; int i = 0; while (i < 10) { a[i] = i; i = i + 1; } int *p = a; return *(p + 7); }"
@}

\section{Known Divergences}
\label{sec:difftest-gaps}

The sweep's last open construct---a \texttt{struct} returned \emph{by
value}---has now been lowered (\S\ref{sec:struct-return}) and graduated
to the real \texttt{sret-*} tests above, following the same path
\texttt{goto} (\S\ref{sec:ir-goto}) and multi-dimensional arrays
(\S\ref{sec:ir-subscript}) took once implemented.  No expected
divergence remains: every program the corpus exercises either agrees
with clang or is an honest feature gap that \kcc{} refuses to compile.
The \texttt{difftest\_xfail} harness stays in place so the next
discovered divergence has a home.

@d Difftest: gaps @{
echo ""
echo "--- Known Divergences (Stage 9 features) ---"
echo "  (none — the struct return-by-value divergence has been resolved)"
@}

\section{Results}

The suite fails only on a mismatch---a genuine wrong answer.  Gaps
(features \kcc{} cannot yet compile) and expected divergences
(\S\ref{sec:difftest-gaps}) are tallied and reported so both
frontiers stay visible, but neither fails the build.

@d Difftest: results @{
echo ""
echo "======================================"
printf "diff results: %d ok, %d mismatch, %d gap, %d xfail (of %d)\n" \
    "$PASS" "$FAIL" "$GAP" "$XFAIL" "$TOTAL"
if [ "$FAIL" -ne 0 ]; then
    exit 1
fi
@}
